Mamba "align" prefix caching: honour `drop_eagle_block` so the state snapshot on
the generation point is not handed back (upstream vllm#53912, #43650).

`MambaManager.find_longest_cache_hit` takes `drop_eagle_block` and never reads
it. `FullAttentionManager` uses it to hand back one drop unit LESS than the
generation point (`hit_length -= min(alignment_tokens, block_size)`); the mamba
finder returns its longest match regardless. The hybrid coordinator reconciles
per-group hits by taking the minimum, so a mamba group that matches further than
the attention groups keeps the resume position, and the request resumes from a
mamba state block that sits on a boundary EAGLE requires be recomputed.

A mamba state block is `[null, ..., state]` and cannot be trimmed after a match
the way append-only attention KV can, so the search range has to stop one unit
short instead. The unit is the one the finder searches in: a hash block in the
fine-grained branch, a cache block in the coarse branch. The coarse half is
upstream #43650 verbatim. The fine half is what that PR misses: the patch lands
below the `return` in the fine-grained branch, so any deployment using
`--prefix-match-unit` below the mamba page size never reaches it (reported on
#53912).

This config takes the fine branch: `block_size` 2176 (the platform raises the
attention block to the mamba page) with `--prefix-match-unit 128`, so
`alignment_tokens` is 128 and `MambaManager` returns early from the fine path.

Why it matters here. The mamba finder registers a "partial tail" state block at
the prompt's last hash boundary (`_cache_partial_tail_block`), which is by
construction the generation point. On the DFlash2/hybrid-GDN target a reused
prefix that ends there produces a silent, low-rate corruption: one stray token
followed by a wall of `!` until the token budget runs out, with spec-decode
acceptance pinned at exactly 0.000 (against 50-80% healthy). Upstream #53912
reports the same "run of repeated `!` characters" at 0.8-0.95% of responses,
scaling with prefix-cache hit rate; #55357 has the same 0.000-acceptance
fingerprint.

Evidence (this box, `[mamba-hit-probe]` on the live tree, CTX=huge):

    block_size=2176 align=128 max_length=86656 max_units=677 drop_eagle=True
      fine_idx=676 hit=86656 blk_aligned=False

— a fine-grained snapshot inside the last block, on the generation point, being
handed back to a request that then resumed there.

Cost. The resumed prefix is one unit shorter, so one unit is recomputed. With
dense checkpoints that is one mamba block (2176 tokens); with sparse retention
(`--prefix-cache-retention-interval 13056`) the next older retained snapshot can
be up to one segment back, so the tail loss is larger. Measured over the
session replay (turns 7-8, six steps): 86,656 -> 78,336 on the worst step
(-8,320, i.e. 9.5% of an 87k prompt, one retained segment) and -256 on another;
the other four steps unchanged.

Retires when the pin carries #43650 (and, for the fine-grained half, whatever
lands on #53912).
